REASON: The diagnosis is right. date_trunc on a timestamptz and the date-to-timestamptz casts in the comparisons both use the session TimeZone, which is UTC on Supabase, so month boundaries fall at 08:00 MYT. The seeded transfer at 2026-03-01 00:30 MYT is 2026-02-28 16:30Z, so it lands in February. The 'from' and 'to' boundary scenarios are also correct. The stated result is wrong, though: this query cannot return a 0 entry, because empty months are dropped (item 9). The real output is two rows, 2026-01: 300000 and 2026-02: 200000, with no March row. Reword so the example matches what the listed code actually returns. 
SUGG: Scenario: the seeded bank transfer received 2026-03-01 00:30 MYT is 2026-02-28 16:30 UTC, so it is counted in February. For 2026-01..2026-03 the query returns [{2026-01, 300000}, {2026-02, 200000}] and no March row, instead of [300000, 0, 200000]. 

15 
REASON: The arithmetic is right: unpaid*2/100 < 0.5 exactly when unpaid < 25, and Math.round(0.5)=1 at 25. But with line 31 as written, an invoice with < 25 sen unpaid only gets past the 0.995 threshold when its amount is under RM 50, for example a one-day prorated last period. For normal rent the case cannot happen. It becomes common once item 13 is fixed. Say so, so the scenario is reachable as stated. 
SUGG: Low - a fee can round to 0 sen (line 32). With line 31 as written this only happens on invoices under RM 50 (e.g. a short prorated last period of RM 10.00 with 20 sen unpaid: 0.98 < 0.995, fee = round(0.4) = 0). Once item 13 is fixed, any invoice with 1-24 sen unpaid hits it. The route then creates a 0-sen late_fee invoice, or, if amount_sen > 0 is enforced, the create fails mid-Promise.all after other fees were committed. Fix: skip fees that round to 0. 

